The Security Council,
Recalling its resolution 2282 (2016) and General Assembly resolution 70/262, as well as its resolution 2413 (2018), and the statements of its President S/PRST/2017/27 of 21 December 2017 and S/PRST/2018/1 of 18 January 2018,
Reaffirming the purposes and principles of the Charter of the United Nations, and reaffirming the primary responsibility of the Security Council for the maintenance of international peace and security, as well as respect for the sovereignty, territorial integrity, and political independence of all States,
Reaffirming the primary responsibility of national Governments and authorities in identifying, driving, and directing priorities, strategies, and activities for peacebuilding and sustaining peace, while acknowledging the important role of the United Nations system and international, regional, subregional, and other partners in support of national efforts, in accordance with national priorities and with the consent of the host State,
Taking note of the 2025 review of the United Nations peacebuilding architecture and the ongoing deliberations thereon, and reaffirming the need for full, effective, and accelerated implementation of the peacebuilding and sustaining peace agenda,
Recognizing that sustaining peace is a shared responsibility of the Security Council, the General Assembly, the Economic and Social Council, the Peacebuilding Commission, and other relevant United Nations organs, and that it requires an integrated and coherent approach across the United Nations system, including peace, development, human rights, humanitarian, and rule-of-law actors,
Emphasizing the importance of conflict prevention, early warning, mediation, good offices, inclusive dialogue, reconciliation, institution-building, and the peaceful settlement of disputes, and the need for adequate, predictable, and sustainable financing for peacebuilding, including from assessed and voluntary contributions, and enhanced partnerships,
Reaffirming the important role of women and youth in peacebuilding and sustaining peace, and the need for their full, equal, meaningful, and safe participation and leadership, and recognizing the contributions of civil society, community leaders, religious actors, and the private sector, in accordance with national laws and policies,
Taking note of the adverse effects of climate change, environmental degradation, food insecurity, and other complex challenges on peace and security, and emphasizing the need for conflict-sensitive and risk-informed approaches, as appropriate and in accordance with existing mandates,
Welcoming the work of the Peacebuilding Commission in providing advice and support to countries emerging from conflict, and the role of the Peacebuilding Fund, and noting the need to strengthen linkages between the Commission, the Security Council, the General Assembly, and the Economic and Social Council,

1. Reaffirms the importance of the full, effective, and accelerated implementation of resolution 2282 (2016) and General Assembly resolution 70/262, and of the peacebuilding and sustaining peace agenda, and encourages all relevant actors to continue to take concrete steps in this regard;

2. Stresses the need for an integrated and coherent approach across the United Nations system, including through the Peacebuilding Commission, the Peacebuilding Support Office, and the Peacebuilding Fund, and requests the Secretary-General to continue to strengthen coherence, coordination, and accountability within existing mandates and resources;

3. Encourages Member States, the United Nations system, regional and subregional organizations, international financial institutions, and other partners to enhance partnerships and support nationally owned and nationally led peacebuilding priorities, including through inclusive dialogue, conflict prevention, institution-building, the rule of law, the promotion and protection of human rights, and socio-economic development, with full respect for national sovereignty and non-interference in the internal affairs of States;

4. Encourages the Secretary-General and all relevant actors to ensure that peacebuilding and sustaining peace efforts are conflict-sensitive and gender-responsive, and include the full, equal, meaningful, and safe participation of women and youth in peacebuilding processes, in accordance with relevant resolutions and national circumstances;

5. Requests the Secretary-General to continue to provide timely and substantive advice and information to the Council on peacebuilding and sustaining peace, including on the implementation of the present resolution and on lessons learned and good practices, within existing mandates and reporting cycles, and to coordinate with the Peacebuilding Commission, as appropriate;

6. Invites the Peacebuilding Commission to continue to provide advice to the Security Council on countries and regions under its consideration, in accordance with its mandate and in full respect of national ownership, and to strengthen its advisory, bridging, and convening roles;

7. Decides to remain seized of the matter.

