Taking note of the adverse effects of environmental degradation, food insecurity, terrorism, violent extremism, transnational organized crime and other complex challenges on peace and security, and emphasizing the need for conflict-sensitive and risk-informed approaches, as appropriate and in accordance with existing mandates,
Welcoming the work of the Peacebuilding Commission in providing advice and support to countries emerging from conflict, and the role of the Peacebuilding Fund, and noting the need to strengthen linkages between the Commission, the Security Council, the General Assembly, and the Economic and Social Council,

1. Reaffirms the importance of the full, effective, and accelerated implementation of resolution 2282 (2016) and General Assembly resolution 70/262, and of the peacebuilding and sustaining peace agenda, and encourages all relevant actors to continue to take concrete steps in this regard;

2. Stresses the need for an integrated and coherent approach across the United Nations system, including through the Peacebuilding Commission, the Peacebuilding Support Office, and the Peacebuilding Fund, and requests the Secretary-General to continue to strengthen coherence, coordination, and accountability within existing mandates and resources, while respecting the respective mandates of the principal organs of the United Nations and the intergovernmental nature of the Organization, including by ensuring timely and flexible access to the Peacebuilding Fund for nationally identified priorities;

3. Calls upon Member States, the United Nations system, international financial institutions and other partners to scale up adequate, predictable, flexible and sustainable financing for nationally led peacebuilding priorities, including through increased voluntary contributions to the Peacebuilding Fund, making better use of existing resources, and enhanced partnerships, while respecting the respective mandates of United Nations organs and the budgetary authority of the General Assembly and without creating new assessed obligations;

4. Encourages Member States, the United Nations system, the African Union, IGAD and other regional and subregional organizations, international financial institutions, and other partners to enhance partnerships and support nationally owned and nationally led peacebuilding priorities, provided without political conditionalities or preconditions, including through inclusive dialogue, conflict prevention, inclusive and accountable institution-building, the rule of law, the promotion and protection of human rights on the basis of equality and mutual respect and in accordance with national laws, legislation, priorities and circumstances, including the right to development, and socio-economic development, with full respect for national sovereignty and non-interference in the internal affairs of States;

5. Encourages the Secretary-General and all relevant actors to ensure that peacebuilding and sustaining peace efforts are conflict-sensitive, gender-sensitive and responsive to the specific needs of women and girls, and include the full, equal, meaningful and safe participation and leadership of women, youth and civil society at all stages of peacebuilding processes, including by preventing and responding to sexual and gender-based violence, in accordance with relevant resolutions, national legislation, priorities and circumstances;

6. Requests the Secretary-General to continue to provide timely and substantive advice and information to the Council on peacebuilding and sustaining peace, including on the implementation of the present resolution, financing for peacebuilding, and lessons learned and good practices, and to include, as appropriate, recommendations on predictable and sustainable financing for peacebuilding, including through voluntary contributions and enhanced partnerships, within existing mandates, reporting cycles and resources, and to coordinate with the Peacebuilding Commission, as appropriate;

7. Invites the Peacebuilding Commission to continue to provide advice to the Security Council on countries and regions under its consideration, in accordance with its mandate and in full respect of national ownership, and to strengthen its advisory, bridging, and convening roles, including in transition contexts and in relation to the drawdown and withdrawal of peace operations;

8. Decides to remain seized of the matter.

